\chapter*{Appendix A: Port-Hamiltonian Derivation}
\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Appendix A: Port-Hamiltonian Derivation}

\section*{A.1 Preliminaries and Physical Foundations}

We begin with the one-dimensional incompressible fluid equations governing hydraulic head 
$H(x,t)$ and volumetric flow rate $Q(x,t)$ in a capillary conduit of cross-sectional area $A$:

\begin{align}
\frac{\partial H}{\partial t} + \frac{1}{A}\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} &= 0, \\
\frac{\partial Q}{\partial t} + A g \frac{\partial H}{\partial x} &= -\frac{f}{2DA^2} Q|Q| + F(x,t),
\end{align}

where $f$ is the Darcy friction factor, $D$ the hydraulic diameter, and $F(x,t)$ the telemetry forcing 
from Face B.

To embed these equations in a Hamiltonian framework, we define canonical coordinates

\[
q(x,t) = H(x,t), \qquad p(x,t) = Q(x,t),
\]

and construct the Hamiltonian functional representing total stored energy:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}(q,p)
= \int_{\Omega}
\left[
\frac{1}{2A\rho} p^2
+ \frac{1}{2}\rho g A q^2
\right] dx.
\label{eq:Hamiltonian}
\end{equation}

The variational derivatives of $\mathcal{H}$ are:

\begin{align}
\frac{\delta \mathcal{H}}{\delta q} &= \rho g A q, \\
\frac{\delta \mathcal{H}}{\delta p} &= \frac{p}{A\rho}.
\end{align}

\section*{A.2 Canonical Hamiltonian Structure}

The conservative (inviscid, unforced) system satisfies the canonical Hamiltonian equations:

\begin{align}
\frac{\partial q}{\partial t} &= \frac{\delta \mathcal{H}}{\delta p}, \\
\frac{\partial p}{\partial t} &= -\frac{\delta \mathcal{H}}{\delta q}.
\end{align}

In matrix form:

\[
\begin{bmatrix}
\partial_t q \\
\partial_t p
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
0 & 1 \\
-1 & 0
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\frac{\delta \mathcal{H}}{\delta q} \\
\frac{\delta \mathcal{H}}{\delta p}
\end{bmatrix}.
\]

This matrix is the canonical symplectic operator $J$ satisfying $J^T = -J$ and $J^2 = -I$.

\section*{A.3 Incorporating Dissipation: The Port-Hamiltonian Extension}

To incorporate hydraulic friction, we introduce the dissipation operator

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}(q,p)
= \frac{f |p|}{2 D A^2 \rho},
\end{equation}

which is positive semi-definite and ensures energy decay.

The extended operator becomes:

\[
J_R =
\begin{bmatrix}
0 & 1 \\
-1 & -\mathcal{R}(q,p)
\end{bmatrix}.
\]

The port-Hamiltonian system is therefore:

\begin{equation}
\begin{bmatrix}
\partial_t q \\
\partial_t p
\end{bmatrix}
=
J_R
\begin{bmatrix}
\frac{\delta \mathcal{H}}{\delta q} \\
\frac{\delta \mathcal{H}}{\delta p}
\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
0 \\
F(x,t)
\end{bmatrix}.
\label{eq:portHamiltonian}
\end{equation}

\section*{A.4 Verification of Port-Hamiltonian Properties}

\subsection*{Skew-Symmetry of the Conservative Component}

The conservative part of $J_R$ is

\[
J =
\begin{bmatrix}
0 & 1 \\
-1 & 0
\end{bmatrix},
\]

which satisfies $J^T = -J$.

\subsection*{Dissipation Inequality}

Energy dissipation satisfies:

\[
\frac{d\mathcal{E}}{dt}
= -\int_{\Omega} \mathcal{R}(q,p) p^2 \, dx
+ \int_{\Omega} p F(x,t) \, dx.
\]

Since $\mathcal{R}(q,p) \ge 0$, the system is passive and dissipative.

\section*{A.5 Final Port-Hamiltonian Operator}

The complete operator governing DF-HITSE is:

\[
\boxed{
\begin{bmatrix}
\partial_t q \\
\partial_t p
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
0 & 1 \\
-1 & -\mathcal{R}(q,p)
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\rho g A q \\
\frac{p}{A\rho}
\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
0 \\
F(x,t)
\end{bmatrix}
}
\]

This operator forms the mathematical backbone of the DF-HITSE architecture, enabling 
symplectic integration, stability analysis, and crypto-hydraulic coupling.

\section*{A.6 Summary}

The port-Hamiltonian derivation demonstrates that DF-HITSE is:

\begin{itemize}
\item structurally conservative in its Hamiltonian core,
\item dissipative under Darcy friction,
\item externally actuated by telemetry forcing,
\item mathematically compatible with symplectic integrators,
\item stable under Lyapunov-bounded energy conditions.
\end{itemize}

This establishes the foundation for all subsequent chapters in the monograph.